{"h": {"measure": "A", "k": 100, "control": "S"}, "sp": "nb", "rep": 131, "hyps": [{"edit_edge": 1, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 5, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "lower", "why": "With edge 5 removed, node 2 can only reach the output through edge 5's absence, so a new conv on 0->2 adds cost without a useful output path; experiments 40-42, 86 and 88 all show large drops versus milder effects when edge 5 is active."}, {"edit_edge": 2, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 5, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "lower", "why": "A conv into node 2 only helps if node 2 feeds the output via edge 5, so the edit should be worse when edge 5 is none."}, {"edit_edge": 3, "edit_op": "none", "cond_edge": 5, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "lower", "why": "Removing the 0->3 shortcut hurts more when edge 5 is also cut and output paths are scarce; experiment 25 is negative while experiments 27-29 are positive."}, {"edit_edge": 3, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 5, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "lower", "why": "Experiments 98 and 100 versus 97 and 99 suggest replacing the shortcut with a conv is worse when edge 5 is missing, with especially large ImageNet16 drops."}, {"edit_edge": 0, "edit_op": "none", "cond_edge": 4, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "higher", "why": "When edge 4 is none, node 1 contributes less to the output, so cutting 0->1 costs little; experiments 31, 33, 36, 81 and 83 are near zero or positive, while 32, 34, 35, 82 and 84 are negative, especially on ImageNet16."}, {"edit_edge": 0, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 4, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "lower", "why": "Strengthening edge 0 matters less when node 1's direct link to the output (edge 4) is absent."}, {"edit_edge": 4, "edit_op": "avg_pool_3x3", "cond_edge": 0, "cond_op": "skip_connect", "direction": "higher", "why": "Experiments 49 and 50 show large gains for pooling on edge 4 when edge 0 is skip, compared with small or negative effects when edge 0 is a conv or none."}, {"edit_edge": 4, "edit_op": "none", "cond_edge": 5, "cond_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "direction": "higher", "why": "Pruning the redundant 1->3 conv helps when edge 5 already supplies a conv path to the output (experiments 90 and 92 versus 89 and 91)."}, {"edit_edge": 3, "edit_op": "skip_connect", "cond_edge": 3, "cond_op": "avg_pool_3x3", "direction": "higher", "why": "Replacing a pooling op on the 0->3 shortcut with an identity restores the clean residual path, giving consistent gains (experiments 18, 22 and 80)."}, {"edit_edge": 2, "edit_op": "none", "cond_edge": 0, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "lower", "why": "When edge 0 is none, node 2's inputs are already sparse, and removing edge 2 further weakens the cell (experiments 93 and 94 versus 95)."}], "tok": 17901, "bad": 0, "probes": [[["nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3", "skip_connect", "nor_conv_3x3", "none"], [5, "nor_conv_3x3"]], [["nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3", "skip_connect", "nor_conv_3x3", "skip_connect"], [5, "nor_conv_3x3"]], [["nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3", "skip_connect", "nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_1x1"], [5, "nor_conv_3x3"]], [["nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3", "skip_connect", "nor_conv_3x3", "avg_pool_3x3"], [5, "nor_conv_3x3"]], [["nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3", "none", "nor_conv_3x3", "none"], [5, "nor_conv_3x3"]], [["nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3", "none", "nor_conv_3x3", "skip_connect"], [5, "nor_conv_3x3"]], [["nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3", "none", "nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_1x1"], [5, "nor_conv_3x3"]], [["nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3", "none", "nor_conv_3x3", "avg_pool_3x3"], [5, "nor_conv_3x3"]], [["nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3", "skip_connect", "nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3"], [5, "none"]], [["nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3", "none", "nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3"], [5, "none"]], [["nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv